  <h2>Notice before the rule</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>A fictional lunch-counter order</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">“Hi. I’d like a sandwich and an apple, please. Can I have some water, too?”</span><br /><span class="ex">“Of course. Would you like a cup or a bottle of water?”</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Which phrases present one complete menu item? Which phrase presents an unspecified amount?</li>
      <li>Why does <span class="ex">an apple</span> use <span class="ex">an</span>? Say the first sound in <em>apple</em>.</li>
      <li>The server asks for a unit: <span class="ex">a cup</span> or <span class="ex">a bottle</span>. What does that unit let us count?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You selected an item, amount, or menu serving from the meaning in each fictional order.">
    <h3>Discovery check: choose the frame that matches the order</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Use <b>a/an</b> for one countable item or named menu serving. Use <b>some</b> when the order presents an unspecified amount.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Food frame choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a">a</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="an">an</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="some">some</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The ticket lists one complete sandwich: I’d like <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a" data-hint="One sandwich is a singular countable item and begins with /s/." data-fix="I’d like a sandwich." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> sandwich.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The ticket lists one whole apple: Can I have <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="an" data-hint="One apple is countable and begins with a vowel sound." data-fix="Can I have an apple?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> apple?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The meeting table has a pitcher, and the guest wants an unspecified amount: I’d like <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="some" data-hint="The pitcher provides an amount of water, not a named serving." data-fix="I’d like some water." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> water.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The plate needs an unspecified amount beside the vegetables: Can I have <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="some" data-hint="Rice is presented as an amount on the plate." data-fix="Can I have some rice?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> rice?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The menu sells one prepared side salad: I’d like <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a" data-hint="Here salad means one complete menu serving." data-fix="I’d like a salad." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> salad.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The cafe menu uses “coffee” for one cup-sized serving: I’d like <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a" data-hint="In this service context, a coffee means one menu serving." data-fix="I’d like a coffee." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> coffee.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Get to the root: countability is a language viewpoint</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>English does not label the physical food once and for all. The noun frame shows how the speaker wants the listener to see it: as one whole item, an amount, or a measured serving.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>viewpoint</th><th>language frame</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>one countable item</b></td><td><b>a/an + singular noun</b></td><td><span class="ex">a sandwich · an egg</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>two or more items</b></td><td><b>number + plural noun</b></td><td><span class="ex">two sandwiches · three eggs</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>an unspecified amount</b></td><td><b>some + amount noun</b></td><td><span class="ex">some bread · some rice</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>a measured or service unit</b></td><td><b>a/an + unit + of + food</b></td><td><span class="ex">a slice of bread · a bowl of rice</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">The dedicated <a href="/lessons/a1/some-any-with-countable-and-uncountable-nouns/">some and any lesson</a> teaches the full quantity system. This lesson applies that system to portions, menus, and service conversations.</p>

  <h3>The same food can enter different frames</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>food</th><th>amount or substance</th><th>countable serving or whole</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>coffee</b></td><td><span class="ex">some coffee</span></td><td><span class="ex">a coffee · two coffees</span> in a service context</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>cake</b></td><td><span class="ex">some cake</span></td><td><span class="ex">a cake · a slice of cake</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>pizza</b></td><td><span class="ex">some pizza</span></td><td><span class="ex">a pizza · a slice of pizza</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>salad</b></td><td><span class="ex">some salad</span></td><td><span class="ex">a salad</span> as one prepared serving</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>chicken</b></td><td><span class="ex">some chicken</span> as food</td><td><span class="ex">a chicken</span> as one animal</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Meaning comes before the article. Do not memorize <em>coffee is uncountable</em> or <em>salad is countable</em> as an absolute rule. Ask what the speaker is presenting in this situation.</p>

  <h3>Useful portions and containers</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>unit</th><th>natural chunk</th><th>what it counts</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>piece / slice</b></td><td><span class="ex">a piece of bread · a slice of pizza</span></td><td>one separated part</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>bowl</b></td><td><span class="ex">a bowl of soup · two bowls of rice</span></td><td>the serving container</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>cup / glass</b></td><td><span class="ex">a cup of coffee · a glass of water</span></td><td>one drink serving</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>bottle / can / carton</b></td><td><span class="ex">a bottle of water · a can of soda · a carton of milk</span></td><td>one package</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Pluralize the unit, not the amount noun: <span class="ex">two slices of bread</span>, <span class="ex">three cups of coffee</span>, and <span class="ex">four bottles of water</span>.</p>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. Each order now uses the exact item number, portion, or container stated on its ticket.">
    <h3>Portion check: complete the ticket</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Portion choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a piece of">a piece of</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a bowl of">a bowl of</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a bottle of">a bottle of</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="two">two</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The ticket requests one individual piece, not a whole loaf: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a piece of" data-hint="A piece makes one countable unit from the amount noun bread." data-fix="The ticket requests a piece of bread." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> bread.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The menu serves rice in one bowl: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a bowl of" data-hint="The bowl is the one countable serving unit." data-fix="The ticket requests a bowl of rice." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> rice.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The cooler ticket requests one sealed water container: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a bottle of" data-hint="A sealed water container is a bottle of water." data-fix="The ticket requests a bottle of water." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> water.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The order card shows the number 2 before a countable menu item: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="two" data-hint="Use the stated number and the plural noun sandwiches." data-fix="The ticket requests two sandwiches." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> sandwiches.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Preference or request? One small sound changes the action</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>form</th><th>meaning</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>I like + food</b></td><td>general preference</td><td><span class="ex">I like coffee.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>I’d like + order</b></td><td>present polite request; <span class="ex">I’d = I would</span></td><td><span class="ex">I’d like a coffee, please.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Can I have + order?</b></td><td>ordinary polite request</td><td><span class="ex">Can I have a glass of water?</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Would you like + offer?</b></td><td>offer something to another person</td><td><span class="ex">Would you like some tea?</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>For an offer, natural short responses are <span class="ex">Yes, please</span> and <span class="ex">No, thank you</span>. At a U.S. counter, staff may ask <span class="ex">For here or to go?</span> Answer <span class="ex">For here, please</span> or <span class="ex">To go, please</span>.</p>

  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>A compact counter exchange</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">Customer: Hi. I’d like a vegetable sandwich and a bottle of water, please.</span><br /><span class="ex">Server: Of course. Would you like some fruit?</span><br /><span class="ex">Customer: No, thank you. That’s all.</span><br /><span class="ex">Server: For here or to go?</span><br /><span class="ex">Customer: To go, please.</span></p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Sound natural: contraction, linking, and information stress</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">I’d</span> is one syllable /aɪd/. Keep the opening light and make the order clear: <span class="ex">I’d like a <strong>SAND</strong>wich, please.</span> In connected U.S. speech, <span class="ex">Can I</span> often links closely, and <span class="ex">Would you</span> can sound like <span class="ex">wouldja</span>. These are listening guides, not spellings to write.</p>
    <p>Link the portion as one chunk: <span class="ex">a-cup-of <strong>COF</strong>fee · a-bottle-of <strong>WA</strong>ter</span>. Keep plural endings audible: <span class="ex">drinks</span> ends /s/, <span class="ex">eggs</span> ends /z/, and <span class="ex">sandwiches</span> adds a final syllable /ɪz/.</p>
    <p>Stress the changed choice in a repair: <span class="ex">No <strong>MILK</strong>, please</span> or <span class="ex">For <strong>HERE</strong>, please</span>. Common food pronunciations include <span class="ex"><strong>VEJ</strong>-tuh-buhl</span>, <span class="ex"><strong>CHOC</strong>-lit</span>, and <span class="ex"><strong>BREK</strong>-fuhst</span>.</p>
  </div>

  <section data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read preference, order, portion, amount, and U.S. service-choice listening check">
